steps.body:
Someone holding puts on a stock that just crashed faces a choice every morning: take the money now, or keep the option and wait. To **exercise** an option is to use the right it gives: for a put, sell the share at K now and collect `K - S`. A European put can only do that at expiry. An American put can do it at any step, so at every node of the tree its holder takes the better of two values, holding it one more step or exercising now:

```cpp
// One node of a put struck at 100, a quarter before expiry, with the stock at 60.
const double K = 100.0, S = 60.0, r = 0.05, sigma = 0.20, dt = 0.25;
const double u = std::exp(sigma * std::sqrt(dt)), d = 1.0 / u;
const double p = (std::exp(r * dt) - d) / (u - d);
const double hold = std::exp(-r * dt) * (p * (K - S * u) + (1 - p) * (K - S * d));
const double exercise = K - S;
std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4) << hold << ' ' << exercise << ' ' << std::max(hold, exercise) << '\n';
```

Holding is worth 38.76, exercising 40, so the holder exercises. In the tree that's one `std::max` per node, and since `std::max` can only raise a value, an American option is worth at least its European twin. The difference is the **early-exercise premium**.

Why give up the time left? The most a put can ever pay is K, if the stock falls to 0. Deep in the money, with the stock far below K, there's little more to gain by waiting, and a lot to lose: K collected now earns interest until expiry, and K collected at expiry doesn't. Here holding is worth exactly `K e^(-r dt) - S`, since the put pays at both children: the strike collected a quarter later, minus the stock. A European put can't exercise before expiry, so it can be worth less than `K - S`. An American put never can, or you'd buy it, exercise at once, and pocket the difference.

A call on a stock that pays no dividend is the opposite case: never exercise it early. Holding it is worth at least `S - K e^(-rT)`, the lower bound that the implied volatility solver of *Black-Scholes, the Greeks, and Implied Volatility* checked, and that's more than the `S - K` that exercising pays whenever r is above 0. So an American call is worth exactly its European price. A **dividend**, cash a company pays its shareholders, changes that: the share drops by the dividend when it's paid, and exercising just before the drop can be worth it.

